# Cursor AI Coding Rules

## TypeScript Strict Typing Rules

### NEVER use `any` type
- Always define proper interfaces or types
- Use `unknown` if the type is truly unknown, then narrow it with type guards
- Use generics when dealing with reusable components
- When working with Object.entries() or similar, properly type the result:
```typescript
// ❌ BAD
const impactLevel = getEventImpactLevel(type as any);

// ✅ GOOD - Import and use the proper type
import { type EventType } from "@/utils/timeline";
const impactLevel = getEventImpactLevel(type as EventType);
```

### Type Definitions
- All component props must have explicit interfaces
- All function parameters must be typed
- All state variables must have explicit types (especially useState)
- All API responses should have typed interfaces

### Common Patterns

#### State Management
```typescript
// ✅ GOOD
interface FormData {
  field1: string;
  field2: number;
}
const [data, setData] = useState<FormData>({ field1: "", field2: 0 });

// ❌ BAD
const [data, setData] = useState<any>({});
```

#### Event Handlers
```typescript
// ✅ GOOD
const handleChange = (e: React.ChangeEvent<HTMLInputElement>) => {
  // ...
};

// ❌ BAD
const handleChange = (e: any) => {
  // ...
};
```

#### Type Guards
```typescript
// ✅ GOOD
const isBullyingReport = (
  report: AdminBullyingReport | AdminHarassmentReport
): report is AdminBullyingReport => {
  return "victimName" in report;
};

// ❌ BAD
const isBullyingReport = (report: any): report is AdminBullyingReport => {
  return "victimName" in report;
};
```

#### Date Handling
```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Handle both string and Date types
const formatDate = (date: string | Date | null | undefined): string => {
  if (!date) return "";
  const dateStr = date instanceof Date ? date.toISOString() : date.toString();
  return dateStr.split("T")[0];
};

// ❌ BAD - Assumes only string
const formatDate = (date: string): string => {
  return date.split("T")[0]; // Will fail if date is Date object
};
```

#### Unknown Types in JSX Conditionals
When using `unknown` types in JSX conditionals, TypeScript cannot infer that the value is safe to render. Use explicit null checks instead of truthy checks.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD - Type error: Type 'unknown' is not assignable to type 'ReactNode'
{event.eventData && (
  <div>{JSON.stringify(event.eventData, null, 2)}</div>
)}

// ✅ GOOD - Use explicit null check to properly narrow the type
{event.eventData != null && (
  <div>{JSON.stringify(event.eventData, null, 2)}</div>
)}

// ✅ ALSO GOOD - Use type guard function for complex cases
const hasEventData = (data: unknown): data is Record<string, unknown> => {
  return data != null && typeof data === "object";
};

{hasEventData(event.eventData) && (
  <div>{JSON.stringify(event.eventData, null, 2)}</div>
)}
```

**Why this matters:** TypeScript's type system requires explicit type narrowing for `unknown` types. Using `!= null` (which checks for both `null` and `undefined`) properly narrows the type and allows TypeScript to understand the value can be used in the conditional.

### Next.js 15 Requirements

#### Dynamic Route Params
```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Next.js 15 requires Promise<> for params
export async function GET(
  request: NextRequest,
  { params }: { params: Promise<{ id: string }> }
) {
  const { id } = await params;
  // use id...
}

// ❌ BAD - Will cause build errors
export async function GET(
  request: NextRequest,
  { params }: { params: { id: string } }
) {
  // accessing params.id without await
}
```

### React JSX Rules

#### Escaped Entities
```typescript
// ✅ GOOD
<p>Click &quot;Start&quot; to begin.</p>
<p>Click {'"Start"'} to begin.</p>

// ❌ BAD
<p>Click "Start" to begin.</p>
```

### File Organization

#### Type Files Location
- `/src/types/` - All shared type definitions
- Component-specific types can be in the same file but should still be properly defined
- API response types should match database schema types

### Prisma/Database Types

When working with Prisma:
1. Always check the schema for actual field names
2. Admin types may differ from form submission types
3. Create separate types for:
   - Form submissions (what users fill)
   - Database models (what Prisma returns)
   - API responses (what gets sent to frontend)

### Build Verification

Before committing:
1. Run `npm run build` to catch all type errors
2. Run `npm run lint` to catch all ESLint errors
3. Fix all TypeScript errors (don't use @ts-ignore unless absolutely necessary)
4. Fix all ESLint errors (warnings can be ignored, but errors must be fixed)
5. Ensure build exits with code 0 before deploying

### React Hooks Best Practices

#### Avoid setState in useEffect
```typescript
// ❌ BAD - Calling setState synchronously in effect
useEffect(() => {
  if (isEditing) {
    setEditName(feature.name); // This triggers cascading renders
  }
}, [isEditing, feature.name]);

// ✅ GOOD - Use initialization pattern or derived state
// Option 1: Initialize state directly with function initializer
const [editName, setEditName] = useState(() => feature.name);

// Option 2: Use key prop to reset component
<Component key={feature.id} feature={feature} />

// Option 3: Use useMemo for derived state
const editName = useMemo(() => feature.name, [feature.name]);

// Option 4: Only setState in response to user actions or external events
useEffect(() => {
  const handleExternalChange = () => {
    setEditName(externalValue); // OK - in callback
  };
  subscribe(handleExternalChange);
  return () => unsubscribe(handleExternalChange);
}, []);

// Option 5: When setState in effect is necessary (e.g., syncing with props on mode change)
// Use eslint-disable with explanation comment
useEffect(() => {
  if (isEditing && !prevIsEditingRef.current) {
    // This is necessary to sync edit state with feature props when user starts editing
    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/set-state-in-effect
    setEditName(feature.name);
  }
  prevIsEditingRef.current = isEditing;
}, [isEditing, feature.name]);
```

**CRITICAL:** The React hooks linter will error on setState in effects. Only use eslint-disable when:
1. Syncing local edit state with props when entering/exiting edit mode
2. Loading from localStorage/external storage
3. Optimistic UI updates for drag-and-drop operations
4. Other legitimate cases where state must sync with external systems

Always add a comment explaining WHY the eslint-disable is necessary.

#### Unused Variables
- Remove unused variables or prefix with underscore if intentionally unused
- Use ESLint disable comment only if variable is needed for type checking
```typescript
// ❌ BAD
const session = await getSession(); // Never used

// ✅ GOOD - Remove if not needed
// Or prefix with underscore if needed for type
const _session = await getSession(); // Intentionally unused
```

#### Variable Declaration (prefer-const)
- Always use `const` for variables that are never reassigned
- Use `let` only when the variable will be reassigned
```typescript
// ❌ BAD
let shouldCreateFile = createNewParam === "true";

// ✅ GOOD
const shouldCreateFile = createNewParam === "true";
```

### Deployment Checklist

**CRITICAL: Before every deployment, verify:**
1. ✅ `npm run build` exits with code 0 (no TypeScript errors)
2. ✅ `npm run lint` has no errors (warnings are acceptable)
3. ✅ All React hooks follow best practices (no setState in effects)
4. ✅ No unused variables that cause lint errors
5. ✅ All imports are used
6. ✅ No console.error or console.warn in production code (use proper error handling)

### Common Deployment Blockers

**These will prevent deployment:**
- TypeScript compilation errors
- ESLint errors (not warnings)
- React hooks violations (setState in effects)
- Unused imports/variables causing errors
- Missing type definitions
- Next.js build failures

**These are acceptable:**
- ESLint warnings (not errors)
- TypeScript warnings
- Style/linting suggestions

### No Hardcoding - Environment & Configuration Rules

**CRITICAL: NEVER hardcode values that could differ between environments or need to be configurable.**

Since code is regularly deployed, hardcoded values can cause production issues, security vulnerabilities, and deployment failures.

#### What NOT to Hardcode

**❌ NEVER hardcode:**
- URLs, API endpoints, or base paths
- API keys, secrets, or tokens
- Database connection strings
- Port numbers
- Environment-specific values (dev/staging/prod)
- Feature flags or toggles
- Timeout values or retry counts
- File paths (especially absolute paths)
- Email addresses or user IDs
- Domain names or hostnames
- Magic numbers or constants that might change

#### What TO Do Instead

**✅ ALWAYS use:**
- Environment variables (`process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_*` for client-side, `process.env.*` for server-side)
- Configuration files (e.g., `config.ts`, `constants.ts` with environment-based values)
- Constants files that read from environment variables
- Next.js environment variable system
- Runtime configuration when possible

#### Examples

```typescript
// ❌ BAD - Hardcoded values
const API_URL = "https://api.example.com/v1";
const API_KEY = "sk-1234567890abcdef";
const TIMEOUT = 5000;
const MAX_RETRIES = 3;
const ADMIN_EMAIL = "admin@example.com";

// ✅ GOOD - Environment variables
const API_URL = process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL || "";
const API_KEY = process.env.API_KEY || "";
const TIMEOUT = Number(process.env.API_TIMEOUT) || 5000;
const MAX_RETRIES = Number(process.env.MAX_RETRIES) || 3;
const ADMIN_EMAIL = process.env.ADMIN_EMAIL || "";

// ✅ GOOD - Configuration file
// config/api.ts
export const apiConfig = {
  url: process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL || "",
  timeout: Number(process.env.API_TIMEOUT) || 5000,
  maxRetries: Number(process.env.MAX_RETRIES) || 3,
};

// ✅ GOOD - Constants file with env-based values
// constants/index.ts
export const APP_CONFIG = {
  apiUrl: process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL || "",
  environment: process.env.NODE_ENV || "development",
  isProduction: process.env.NODE_ENV === "production",
} as const;
```

#### File Paths

```typescript
// ❌ BAD - Hardcoded absolute paths
const filePath = "/home/user/projects/impactly/uploads/file.txt";
const uploadDir = "/var/www/uploads";

// ✅ GOOD - Use relative paths or environment variables
const filePath = path.join(process.cwd(), "uploads", "file.txt");
const uploadDir = process.env.UPLOAD_DIR || path.join(process.cwd(), "uploads");
```

#### API Endpoints

```typescript
// ❌ BAD - Hardcoded endpoints
const response = await fetch("https://api.example.com/v1/users/123");

// ✅ GOOD - Use environment variables or config
const baseUrl = process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL || "";
const response = await fetch(`${baseUrl}/users/123`);
```

#### Feature Flags

```typescript
// ❌ BAD - Hardcoded feature flags
const enableFeature = true;

// ✅ GOOD - Environment-based feature flags
const enableFeature = process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_ENABLE_FEATURE === "true";
```

#### When Hardcoding is Acceptable

**Only hardcode:**
- Mathematical constants (e.g., `Math.PI`, `Math.E`)
- Standard HTTP status codes (e.g., `200`, `404`, `500`) - though enums are better
- CSS class names or component names
- Internal type definitions or interfaces
- Pure mathematical calculations
- Standard library constants

**Even then, prefer:**
- Named constants or enums for better maintainability
- Configuration objects for related values

```typescript
// ✅ ACCEPTABLE - But enum is better
const HTTP_STATUS = {
  OK: 200,
  NOT_FOUND: 404,
  INTERNAL_ERROR: 500,
} as const;

// ✅ BETTER - Use enum
enum HttpStatus {
  OK = 200,
  NOT_FOUND = 404,
  INTERNAL_ERROR = 500,
}
```

#### Pre-Deployment Checklist

Before deploying, verify:
1. ✅ No hardcoded URLs, API keys, or secrets
2. ✅ All environment variables are documented
3. ✅ All environment variables have fallback values where appropriate
4. ✅ Configuration files use environment variables
5. ✅ No absolute file paths (use `process.cwd()` or relative paths)
6. ✅ Feature flags are environment-based

## Summary

**Golden Rules:**
1. NO `any` types - EVER
2. ALL parameters must be typed
3. ALL state must be explicitly typed
4. Next.js 15 dynamic params must be Promise<>
5. Always handle both string and Date types for date fields
6. Run build before committing
7. NEVER hardcode values - Use environment variables and configuration files (critical for regular deployments)

## Project Planning

### Plan Documentation
When creating or updating project plans:

1. **Always save plans externally** to `/plans/` directory
2. **Naming convention:** `NN-descriptive-name.md` (e.g., `01-analytics-plan.md`)
3. **Required sections:**
   - Goal/Overview
   - Implementation phases
   - Success metrics
   - Tech stack decisions
   - Timeline

4. **Version control:** Plans directory is gitignored
5. **Plan format:** Markdown (.md) for readability
6. **Updates:** When plans change, update the .md file

This ensures plans are:
- Persistent across sessions
- Easy to reference and update
- Available for team members
- Separate from code commits

### Documentation & Milestone Files
**CRITICAL:** All documentation and milestone tracking files must go in `/plans/milestones/`, NOT in `/src/utils/` or any source code directory.

**Files that belong in `/plans/milestones/`:**
- Implementation status/progress documents (e.g., `IMPLEMENTATION_STATUS.md`, `REFACTOR_PROGRESS.md`)
- Completion summaries (e.g., `PHASE1_COMPLETE.md`, `IMPLEMENTATION_COMPLETE.md`)
- Migration guides (e.g., `DATABASE_MIGRATION_GUIDE.md`, `DASHBOARD_MIGRATION_COMPLETE.md`)
- Bug fix documentation (e.g., `FOLDER_DELETE_BUG_FIX.md`)
- Build/status tracking files (e.g., `BUILD_STATUS.md`)
- Feature guides (e.g., `ADMIN_ANALYTICS_GUIDE.md`)
- Any other `.md` documentation files that track project milestones, status, or implementation details

**Rules:**
- ✅ **DO:** Place all `.md` documentation files in `/plans/milestones/`
- ❌ **DON'T:** Place documentation files in `/src/utils/` or any source code directory
- The `/src/utils/` directory is for utility functions only, not documentation

This keeps documentation organized and separate from source code.

## Color & Theme Management Rules

**CRITICAL: ALL colors must be managed through the theme system. NEVER hardcode colors in components.**

This app uses a centralized theme system that allows users to customize colors. All color changes must go through theme tokens, not direct color values in component code.

### Theme System Architecture

The app has two levels of color management:

1. **Design Tokens** (`src/styles/design-tokens.ts`):
   - Base color palette (primary, accent, neutral, success, warning, error)
   - Used for semantic color references
   - Provides color scales (50-900) for each color family

2. **Theme Tokens** (`src/components/theme/types.ts`):
   - Runtime theme tokens that users can customize
   - Managed through `ThemeProvider` (`src/components/theme/ThemeProvider.tsx`)
   - Theme presets defined in `src/components/theme/theme-presets.ts`
   - Includes: `background`, `surface`, `card`, `border`, `textPrimary`, `textSecondary`, `button`, `buttonText`, `accent`, etc.

### Rules for Color Changes

**❌ NEVER do this:**
```typescript
// ❌ BAD - Hardcoded colors in components
<button style={{ backgroundColor: "#3B82F6", color: "#ffffff" }}>
  Click me
</button>

// ❌ BAD - Hardcoded colors in CSS/Tailwind
<div className="bg-blue-500 text-white">
  Content
</div>

// ❌ BAD - Direct hex values
const buttonColor = "#4f46e5";
```

**✅ ALWAYS do this:**
```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Use theme tokens from ThemeProvider
import { useTheme } from "@/components/theme/ThemeProvider";

const MyComponent = () => {
  const { tokens } = useTheme();
  
  return (
    <button 
      style={{ 
        backgroundColor: tokens.button, 
        color: tokens.buttonText 
      }}
    >
      Click me
    </button>
  );
};

// ✅ GOOD - Use CSS variables (if theme system exposes them)
<button className="bg-[var(--theme-button)] text-[var(--theme-button-text)]">
  Click me
</button>

// ✅ GOOD - Use design tokens for semantic colors
import { colors } from "@/styles/design-tokens";

const errorColor = colors.error[500]; // Use semantic color scale
```

### Where to Make Color Changes

**For UI element colors (buttons, backgrounds, text, etc.):**
1. Check `src/components/theme/theme-presets.ts` for existing theme presets
2. Update the appropriate token in the preset (e.g., `button`, `buttonText`, `accent`)
3. If adding a new token, update:
   - `src/components/theme/types.ts` (add to `ThemeTokens` interface)
   - `src/components/theme/theme-presets.ts` (add to all presets)
   - `src/components/theme/ThemeProvider.tsx` (if needed for provider logic)

**For base color palette changes:**
1. Update `src/styles/design-tokens.ts`
2. Modify the color scales (primary, accent, neutral, etc.)

**For component-specific color usage:**
1. Import and use theme tokens: `const { tokens } = useTheme()`
2. Or use design tokens: `import { colors } from "@/styles/design-tokens"`
3. Never hardcode hex values, RGB values, or color names

### Accessing Theme Tokens

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Using ThemeProvider hook
import { useTheme } from "@/components/theme/ThemeProvider";

const MyComponent = () => {
  const { tokens } = useTheme();
  
  return (
    <div style={{ 
      backgroundColor: tokens.background,
      color: tokens.textPrimary,
      borderColor: tokens.border
    }}>
      Content
    </div>
  );
};
```

### Common Color Use Cases

**Buttons:**
```typescript
// ✅ GOOD
const { tokens } = useTheme();
<button style={{ 
  backgroundColor: tokens.button, 
  color: tokens.buttonText 
}}>
  Submit
</button>

// ❌ BAD
<button style={{ backgroundColor: "#4f46e5", color: "#ffffff" }}>
  Submit
</button>
```

**Text:**
```typescript
// ✅ GOOD
const { tokens } = useTheme();
<p style={{ color: tokens.textPrimary }}>Primary text</p>
<span style={{ color: tokens.textSecondary }}>Secondary text</span>

// ❌ BAD
<p style={{ color: "#0f172a" }}>Primary text</p>
```

**Backgrounds:**
```typescript
// ✅ GOOD
const { tokens } = useTheme();
<div style={{ backgroundColor: tokens.background }}>
  <div style={{ backgroundColor: tokens.card }}>Card content</div>
</div>

// ❌ BAD
<div style={{ backgroundColor: "#f8fafc" }}>
  <div style={{ backgroundColor: "#ffffff" }}>Card content</div>
</div>
```

**Accents:**
```typescript
// ✅ GOOD
const { tokens } = useTheme();
<div style={{ 
  backgroundColor: tokens.accent, 
  color: tokens.accentContrast 
}}>
  Highlighted content
</div>

// ❌ BAD
<div style={{ backgroundColor: "#6366f1", color: "#ffffff" }}>
  Highlighted content
</div>
```

### Pre-Development Checklist

Before making any color changes:
1. ✅ Identify which theme token should be used (check `ThemeTokens` interface)
2. ✅ Check if the token exists in theme presets
3. ✅ If adding a new color need, add it to the theme system first
4. ✅ Never hardcode colors directly in components
5. ✅ Test with different theme presets to ensure colors work across themes

### Why This Matters

- **User customization**: Users can change themes, so hardcoded colors break this
- **Consistency**: All colors come from a single source of truth
- **Maintainability**: Color changes happen in one place, not scattered across components
- **Accessibility**: Theme system ensures proper contrast ratios
- **Dark mode support**: Theme tokens handle light/dark variants automatically

**Remember: If you need to change a color, change it in the theme system, not in the component code!**

## Theme Change Implementation Rules

**CRITICAL: Theme changes MUST be instant and provide immediate visual feedback. This has been a major pain point - follow these rules exactly.**

### When Implementing Theme Changes

**ALWAYS follow this pattern in `ThemeProvider.tsx`:**

1. **The `applyTokens` function MUST update BOTH root and body:**
```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Updates both root and body
const applyTokens = (tokens: ThemeTokens, base?: "light" | "dark") => {
  const root = document.documentElement;
  const body = document.body;

  // Set CSS variables on root (html) element - these cascade to all children
  Object.entries(tokens).forEach(([token, value]) => {
    root.style.setProperty(`--theme-${token}`, value || "");
  });

  // ALSO update body's inline styles to keep them in sync with theme changes
  // This ensures the background updates immediately, even behind modals
  Object.entries(tokens).forEach(([token, value]) => {
    body.style.setProperty(`--theme-${token}`, value || "");
  });

  // Explicitly update body background to ensure it updates behind modals
  if (tokens.background) {
    body.style.setProperty("background-color", tokens.background);
  }

  if (base) {
    root.style.setProperty("color-scheme", base);
  }
};
```

**❌ NEVER do this:**
```typescript
// ❌ BAD - Only updates root, body won't update instantly
const applyTokens = (tokens: ThemeTokens, base?: "light" | "dark") => {
  const root = document.documentElement;
  Object.entries(tokens).forEach(([token, value]) => {
    root.style.setProperty(`--theme-${token}`, value || "");
  });
  // Missing body updates!
};
```

2. **Theme changes MUST be applied BEFORE state updates for instant feedback:**
```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Apply theme immediately, then update state
const handleSetPreset = (id: string) => {
  const preset = THEME_PRESETS.find((p) => p.id === id);
  if (preset) {
    // Apply theme IMMEDIATELY for instant visual feedback
    applyAndSaveTheme(id, preset.tokens, preset.base);
    // Then update state
    setPresetId(id);
  }
};

// ❌ BAD - State update first, theme applies later (delayed feedback)
const handleSetPreset = (id: string) => {
  setPresetId(id); // State updates first
  // Theme applies in useEffect later - too slow!
};
```

3. **Always dispatch theme change events for component reactivity:**
```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Dispatch event for instant component updates
const dispatchThemeChange = (
  presetId: string,
  tokens: ThemeTokens,
  base?: "light" | "dark"
) => {
  const event = new CustomEvent("themechange", {
    detail: { presetId, tokens, base },
    bubbles: true,
  });
  document.documentElement.dispatchEvent(event);
};

// Always call this after applying tokens
applyTokens(tokens, base);
dispatchThemeChange(presetId, tokens, base);
```

### Loading States and Backgrounds

**When creating loading screens or full-page components, ALWAYS use theme tokens:**

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Uses theme background
import { useTheme } from "@/components/theme/ThemeProvider";

export default function LoadingScreen() {
  const { tokens } = useTheme();
  
  return (
    <div 
      className="min-h-screen flex items-center justify-center"
      style={{ backgroundColor: tokens.background }}
    >
      {/* Content */}
    </div>
  );
}

// ❌ BAD - Hardcoded background that won't match theme
export default function LoadingScreen() {
  return (
    <div className="min-h-screen bg-white flex items-center justify-center">
      {/* Content */}
    </div>
  );
}
```

### Testing Theme Changes

**Before committing theme-related changes:**

1. ✅ Test theme changes in the theme picker - should be INSTANT
2. ✅ Test with different presets - all should work immediately
3. ✅ Test custom token updates - should update instantly
4. ✅ Test loading screens - should match theme background
5. ✅ Test modals/overlays - background should update behind them
6. ✅ Test full-page components - should use theme colors

### Common Mistakes to Avoid

**❌ NEVER:**
- Only update CSS variables on root (must also update body)
- Apply theme changes after state updates (must be before)
- Skip dispatching theme change events
- Use hardcoded colors in loading states
- Forget to update body background-color directly
- Remove the body CSS variable updates (they're critical for modals)

**✅ ALWAYS:**
- Update both `document.documentElement` AND `document.body`
- Apply theme changes synchronously before React state updates
- Dispatch theme change events for component reactivity
- Use `useTheme()` hook in components that need theme colors
- Test theme changes immediately after implementation
- Verify loading screens use theme tokens

### Theme Change Checklist

**When modifying `ThemeProvider.tsx` or theme-related code:**

1. ✅ `applyTokens` updates both root AND body CSS variables
2. ✅ Body background-color is set directly
3. ✅ Theme changes apply BEFORE state updates
4. ✅ Theme change events are dispatched
5. ✅ Loading screens use theme tokens
6. ✅ All components using colors use theme tokens
7. ✅ Tested with multiple theme presets
8. ✅ Tested with custom theme tokens
9. ✅ Verified instant visual feedback

### Why This Matters

- **User Experience**: Instant theme changes feel responsive and professional
- **Visual Consistency**: Body updates ensure modals/overlays match theme
- **Component Reactivity**: Events ensure all components update immediately
- **Maintainability**: Following this pattern prevents future pain points

**CRITICAL: If you modify theme change logic, you MUST test that changes are instant. This has been a major pain point - don't break it!**

